<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commentarii de Bello Gallico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 50s BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Commentaries on the Gallic War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Commentarii de Bello Gallico is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s account of his wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> from 58 to 52 BCE, written in the third person. Besides the campaigns, it describes the peoples of Gaul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, including his two expeditions to Britain in 55 and 54 BCE. Several British peoples, such as the Bibroci and Cassi, are known only from it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commentarii-de-bello-gallico.jpg" alt="Commentarii de Bello Gallico" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caesar, unbekannter Illustrator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesar_bsb_beck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Caesar's victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> had increased the alarm and hostility of his aristocratic enemies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the optimates, who intended to prosecute him for abuse of his authority once he laid down his imperium. A conviction would have stripped him of wealth and citizenship and undone the laws he had passed as consul. The Commentaries are understood as an effort to address the plebeians and the Tribunes of the Plebs directly, bypassing the Senate, and to present the wars as just and pious, fought in self-defence.</p><p>The classicist Ruth Breindal thinks Caesar probably dictated much of the work to a scribe, while keeping an overwhelming hand in it, and regards its main purpose as propaganda to protect his reputation. The last chapters of Book 8 were written after Caesar's death in 44 BCE by the consul Aulus Hirtius, who died in 43 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work opens with the often quoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> est omnis divisa in partes tres, &quot;Gaul is a whole divided into three parts&quot;. It is divided into eight books of roughly 5,000 to 15,000 words. Gaul here means the Celtic regions outside the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> already held. The narrative begins in 58 BCE with the migration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a>, whom neighbouring allies asked Caesar to resist, and draws in further tribes and the Germanic Suebi.</p><p>Among the Gallic leaders, Diviciacus of the Haedui (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>) is shown as the one man in whom Caesar had absolute confidence, and his appeals led Caesar to act against the German leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a>. Book 6 describes the Germans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druid">Druids</a> and their sacrifices. Book 5 tells of the centurions Lucius Vorenus and Titus Pullo, rivals who saved each other's lives when the garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-tullius-cicero">Quintus Tullius Cicero</a> was besieged in a revolt of the Belgae led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most contemporaries and later historians took the account as truthful until the 20th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> historians began to question it, above all its claim that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> faced Gallic forces of up to 430,000, a size thought impossible for an army of the time, and suffered no deaths against them. From the 1970s some critics read the work less as history than as literature.</p><p>Its clear, direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> has made it a mainstay of Latin teaching. About 240 medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> preserve it, the oldest written at Fleury Abbey in the later ninth century, and the first printed edition was published by Giovanni Andrea Bussi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1469. Caesar appears in the Astérix albums, whose jokes include his speaking of himself in the third person.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Commentarii de Bello Gallico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ancalites are known only from <b>Caesar's Gallic War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ancalites">Ancalites</a></li><li><blockquote>Known only from <b>Caesar's Gallic War</b>, the Bibroci were one of five British peoples, with the Cenimagni, Segontiaci, Ancalites and Cassi, who sent envoys to surrender to him in 54 BCE after the Trinovantes had come under his protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/bibroci">Bibroci</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cassi are recorded once in <b>Caesar's Gallic War</b> as one of five British peoples, with the Cenimagni, Segontiaci, Ancalites and Bibroci, who sent envoys to surrender during his second invasion in 54 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/cassi">Cassi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Segontiaci appear once in <b>Caesar's Gallic War</b>, among the peoples who surrendered after the Trinovantes made terms with Rome in 54 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/segontiaci">Segontiaci</a></li></ul></section>
